#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""OpenMower's planner service, slic3r_coverage_planner/plan_path, answered by the MowBite Planner (ROS 1 Noetic).

Started in place of the slic3r planner, mower_logic and the mowing.plan RPC use it as they are. The request
gives what it always did (outline, holes, angle, lane spacing, loops, offset, skip flags), the answer is the
same list of paths (perimeter loops is_outline 1, the lanes with their turns 0), poses 0.1 m apart facing
the next one, each path continuous and at most max_path_poses long.

On top, from OpenMower's map (mower_map_service/json_map) it finds the area the outline belongs to (of two
with it the one that's active and mowable):
  - its "planner" property: any MowBite Planner setting for this area (pattern, turns, angle strategy...),
    and its angle_min/angle_max. With angle or angle_strategy in it the lanes go that way instead of
    mower_logic's angle. mower_map keeps them since open_mower_ros #364, before it they're dropped.
  - its own angle: with one, mower_logic's angle counts even with angle or angle_strategy in settings.
  - the areas to mow around (mow_around) in it, on top of the obstacles mower_logic sends, and with
    nested_areas the other mowing areas lying in it (they get their own plan).
  - the other lawns: the shortest way between parts of the area may go through them. Not the navigation
    areas: mower_logic mows a path from start to end, a part only reachable over them gets paths of its own.
  - the docking station: plans start on the loop next to it and end close to it.
  - an obstacle's (or an area's to keep out of) own "margin" property (m): the body keeps that far off it in place of
    obstacle_margin. The obstacles mower_logic sends are found in the map by their outline.
The plan never depends on where the mower is, so the same map gives the same plan (mower_logic compares a
digest of it to resume an interrupted mow).

With the setting planner slic3r (for all areas) OpenMower's slic3r planner plans instead: the launch file starts it
next to this node with its service moved to classic_service, mower_logic's request goes there as it came and its
answer back as it is (where it doesn't answer the MowBite Planner plans). planner.plan asks it like mower_logic would.

Parameters (private): mower_width (blade, m), settings (dict of MowBite Planner settings for all areas, on top of
lane_order skip for OpenMower's forward only FTC controller), settings_file (where the
settings changed over RPC are kept, on top of these two; default $ROS_HOME/mowbite_planner_settings.json, empty:
until the next start only), plans_dir (where the plans are kept, the last DISK_PLANS of this planner code, default
$ROS_HOME/mowbite_plans, empty: in memory only), max_path_poses (at least 4), frame_id, service, classic_service,
map_topic, use_map, rpc. The request always gives the lane spacing (with mower_width the overlap), perimeter_offset,
perimeter_passes, lane_overlap_passes, the skip flags and a concentric fill_pattern; settings beat the first four (an
area's own outline_count, ... beat those), not the others (an area's planner property can). The angle too, unless
settings has angle or angle_strategy and the area has no angle of its own. Whether it has one comes from the saved
map: a preview in the map editor (mowing.plan with the outline and the edited angle) of an area whose angle was just
added or removed follows the saved area until the edit is saved.

OpenMower RPCs (xbot_monitoring, JSON-RPC over MQTT):
  planner.settings      every setting: value, default (without the stored ones), stored, type, unit, range or
                        choices, settable. Not settable: the skip flags of mower_logic's request.
                        mower_width is the blade, it beats the mower_width parameter (OM_BLADE_WIDTH). robot_outline's
                        type is points ([[x, y], ...]). planner (not a planner setting): mowbite or slic3r.
                        leftovers_auto (the node's too): see below
  planner.settings.set  {name: value, ...}, null: back to the default. Checked as a whole, kept in settings_file,
                        the next plan uses them (an interrupted area whose plan changes starts again). Answers
                        like planner.settings
  planner.angle.reset   back to no steps of angle_increment turned (answers like planner.settings, which says
                        angle_steps, angle_mows: the finished mows towards the next step, angle_turned in rad, and
                        finished_mows: all of them, see perimeter_direction random)
  planner.log           the node's last LOG_LINES log lines since it started: {since (unix s, only the lines after it),
                        limit (only the newest that many)} gives {lines: [{t (unix s), level (INFO, WARNING or ERROR),
                        msg (a traceback with it where there's one)}, ...]}, the oldest first
  planner.drive         how close the body came to the edges driving: {job_id or file (of position_history, default:
                        the latest), area_id (default: every active mowing area)} gives {file, areas: {id: {checked,
                        places: [[x, y, m]] where the body at a driven point got m into the margins (more than
                        DRIVE_OFF; not within DOCK_REACH of a docking station; with the blade off, OpenMower's own
                        drives, the lawns and navigation areas count as well), worst}}}. After every finished
                        mow (JOB_COMPLETE) the same for that job, in the log and as the answer without params,
                        once the job ended and its track is all written (see TRACK_WAIT)
  planner.leftovers     what a mow really left (its track with the blade running against the area, the blade's way
                        between two driven points) and the passes over it: {job_id (all of its position_history files:
                        a restart begins a new one) or file (that one), default: the latest file's job, area_id
                        (default: every active mowing area), poses, arm} gives {file, files,
                        areas: {id: like planner.plan's answer with only those passes (the one nearest the dock
                        first; paths empty and message where there's nothing to do), left_m2: unmowed pieces of 0.02 m2 at
                        least, stats.leftovers: {pieces, passes, area: m2 the passes mow}}}. With more than half of
                        an area left it's no mow of it: no paths, a message, and armed that area is planned as
                        ever. arm true: the next
                        plans mower_logic asks for (within LEFTOVERS_ARMED s) are these, an area with nothing left
                        gets no paths (mower_logic skips it): start mowing as ever and the mower only mows what
                        was left. That job doesn't count as a mow (angle_increment, duration). armed: s it still is
  planner.plan          like ext.mowbite_planner.plan of the MQTT service (area_id or outline, settings to try
                        before setting them, poses...) with the node's settings and mower_logic's parameters.
                        Not in it: the increment mower_logic adds to the angle after every finished job. kept: the
                        plan was there already (planned ahead or asked for before). duration: {min, model_min, factor,
                        runs}, how long mowing it takes, see TIMING_RUNS

leftovers_auto: after every finished mow planner.leftovers by itself, armed. Where its passes mow LEFTOVERS_AUTO_MIN m2
at least the mower is sent out again for them (the start_mowing action), once it's idle in its dock with no emergency
and no rain (rain only counts with /mower_logic/rain_mode on). Once a mow: the leftovers' job starts no other.

PLAN_AHEAD_DELAY s after the map or the settings last changed every active mowing area is planned ahead like
planner.plan plans it, so the app's preview and the next mow find their plan at once.

angle_increment: every angle_increment_every finished mows (mower_logic's JOB_COMPLETE event) the lanes of every plan
turn that much further (angle_steps, kept in mowbite_planner_angle.json next to settings_file), within an area's
angle_min..angle_max. The mows are counted for all areas at once: angle_increment_every is the one set for all, an
area's own doesn't count. mower_logic's own mow_angle_increment is best left at 0, both add up.

perimeter_direction random: the loops go round ccw or cw at random, drawn again after every finished mow (JOB_COMPLETE,
not the leftovers' job) from the count of all of them (finished_mows, kept there too, planner.angle.reset leaves it),
the same for every area: a plan asked for again before the next one finishes (resuming, a preview) goes the same way.

In a container of its own (OM_PLANNER=external in open_mower_ros) the node ends with exit code 1 when the ROS master
restarts (a new /run_id): the new master doesn't know its service and RPC methods (nor the slic3r planner's), with
the launch file's own_container everything ends and the restarted container registers them again. Its restart policy
has to be on (restart: unless-stopped, see compose.yaml).
"""

